1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,584
Welcome to Episode 4, the finale of our Hello World, pg_bio intro series!

2
00:00:04,584 --> 00:00:09,168
Now we unite all layers into the Multiomics Holy Grail:

3
00:00:09,168 --> 00:00:13,752
Joint Vector Spaces and live biological database integration.

4
00:00:13,752 --> 00:00:17,916
In extreme environments like deep-sea hydrothermal vents, evolution mutates an enzyme's amino acid sequence

5
00:00:17,916 --> 00:00:22,080
until less than 25 percent of the letters match known proteins.

6
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:26,244
Traditional sequence search tools like BLAST go blind in this Twilight Zone, even when

7
00:00:26,244 --> 00:00:30,408
the 3D catalytic fold is preserved.

8
00:00:30,408 --> 00:00:37,548
By storing 1280-dimensional ESM-2 protein foundation model embeddings as 16-bit halfvecs indexed with HNSW,

9
00:00:37,548 --> 00:00:44,688
PostgreSQL narrows 2.34 million dark proteins down to the top 15 structural twins in 0.4 milliseconds.

10
00:00:44,688 --> 00:00:50,304
Then the PostgreSQL query planner short-circuits: it runs our compiled Rust Smith-Waterman dynamic programming

11
00:00:50,304 --> 00:00:55,920
and Henderson-Hasselbalch pI calculations on only those 15 survivors, and calls bio_fetch_uniprot via LATERAL

12
00:00:55,920 --> 00:01:01,536
join to pull live organism and taxonomy metadata straight from UniProt into your SQL table.

13
00:01:01,536 --> 00:01:06,096
From 1D DNA intervals, to 3D Morton pockets, to sparse CSR graphs, to HNSW

14
00:01:06,096 --> 00:01:10,656
vector discovery—you now know the core multiomics stack in PostgreSQL.

15
00:01:10,656 --> 00:01:15,216
Dive deeper at ideia dot me and star pg_bio on GitHub!
